Laya-JA — Japanese typed decision adapters.
The code in this project is licensed under Apache-2.0 (LICENSE).
Base model: Convai Innovations, convaiinnovations/laya-multilingual, Apache-2.0.
Upstream engine: NandhaKishorM/laya, Apache-2.0. Base weights are downloaded separately.
The JSON adapters replace the final option-scoring weight matrix.
Optional safetensors files add a trained per-option residual scorer.

Training and benchmark source: JGLUE v1.3, JNLI and JCommonsenseQA,
Yahoo Japan Corporation and Kawahara Lab at Waseda University.
Kurihara, Kawahara and Shibata, JGLUE: Japanese General Language Understanding Evaluation, LREC 2022.
https://github.com/yahoojapan/JGLUE
https://aclanthology.org/2022.lrec-1.317/
Data are obtained from the original repository. No JGLUE text is bundled.
See its README for dataset-specific credits, including YJ Captions and Japanese ConceptNet.
The original Japanese challenge items and fitted adapter files are offered under CC BY-SA 4.0.
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
Attribute this release to the Laya-JA project and retain upstream attributions.

JCoLA v1.0, Japanese Corpus of Linguistic Acceptability.
Taiga Someya, Yushi Sugimoto, Yohei Oseki (2024); CC BY-SA 4.0.
https://github.com/osekilab/JCoLA
https://aclanthology.org/2024.lrec-main.828/
JSTS v1.3 comes from the same JGLUE project cited above.

Additional sources for the multi-corpus experiments (source texts are retrieved separately):
JSICK — Hitomi Yanaka and Koji Mineshima, Compositional Evaluation on Japanese Textual Entailment and Similarity (2022). See upstream LICENSE.txt (CC BY-SA 4.0).
https://github.com/verypluming/JSICK
JaNLI — Hitomi Yanaka and Koji Mineshima, Assessing the Generalization Capacity of Pre-trained Language Models through Japanese Adversarial Natural Language Inference (2021), CC BY-SA 4.0.
https://github.com/verypluming/JaNLI
Jamp — Tomoki Sugimoto, Yasumasa Onoe and Hitomi Yanaka, Jamp: Controlled Japanese Temporal Inference Dataset for Evaluating Generalization Capacity of Language Models (2023), CC BY-SA 4.0.
https://github.com/tomo-ut/temporalNLI_dataset
JSeM — Japanese Semantics test suite, Ai Kawazoe, Ribeka Tanaka, Koji Mineshima and Daisuke Bekki; distributed by the JSeM project, BSD 3-Clause. Evaluation only.
https://github.com/DaisukeBekki/JSeM
JSQuAD — JGLUE contributors, based on Japanese Wikipedia. Experiments construct five candidate answers from a single article. This is a modified task.
https://github.com/yahoojapan/JGLUE/tree/main/datasets/jsquad-v1.3

The grounded-qa head is trained on the JSQuAD/JGLUE source described above, using
five-candidate questions derived from short passages. JSQuAD credits the authors of
Japanese Wikipedia. Only model weights, code, item identifiers and predictions are
redistributed here. The grounded-qa head is provided under CC BY-SA 4.0.

Optional grounded-qa-plus auxiliary model: Skelter Labs, bert-base-japanese-jaquad,
trained on JaQuAD (2022), model card license CC BY-SA 3.0. Its weights are obtained
from the upstream publisher and are not redistributed in this repository.
https://huggingface.co/SkelterLabsInc/bert-base-japanese-jaquad
https://huggingface.co/datasets/SkelterLabsInc/JaQuAD
The new Laya readout weights and generated Japanese logic examples are offered under
CC BY-SA 4.0 under the attributions in this notice.
